\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N=\{1,2,\ldots\}$, and $\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$ denote the integers, rationals, reals and complex numbers. Any other conventions are specified locally.


A full-rank lattice $L=L(B)=B\mathbb Z^n\subset\mathbb R^n$ is specified by a rational basis matrix $B$. Length uses the Euclidean norm. Put $\lambda_1(L)=\min\{\|v\|_2:0\ne v\in L\}$. For a monic $f\in\mathbb Z[X]$ of degree $n$, identify $\mathbb Z[X]/(f)$ with $\mathbb Z^n$ by the coefficient vector of the representative of degree less than $n$. An ideal lattice is the full-rank lattice associated to an ideal of this ring in this coefficient embedding. The case $f=X^n-1$ is cyclic: it is invariant under $(v_1,\ldots,v_n)\mapsto(v_n,v_1,\ldots,v_{n-1})$. Exact SVP asks for a nonzero $v\in L$ of length $\lambda_1(L)$. Exact CVP, given the same type of lattice and a rational target $t\in\mathbb Q^n$, asks for $v\in L$ minimizing $\|v-t\|_2$. A polynomial-time oracle reduction may call a solver for the target problem while doing polynomially many operations in the bit length of its rational input.
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Does SVP admit a polynomial-time reduction to CVP that keeps the oracle lattice inside the class of cyclic lattices? Does such a reduction exist inside the class of ideal lattices? \href{https://helper.ipam.ucla.edu/publications/SCWS1/SCWS1_6240.pdf}{[S2]}

\subsection{Short English statement}
Can the known general shortest-vector-to-closest-vector reduction be carried out while preserving cyclic or ideal structure?

